# Controller Unit Test Cases Rules

## Overview
This document defines the standards and patterns for writing unit test cases for REST controllers in the timesheet microservice. These rules ensure consistency, maintainability, and comprehensive test coverage across all controller tests.

## Test Class Structure

### 1. Test Class Declaration
```java
@SpringBootTest(classes = TimesheetApplication.class)
class [Controller]Tests {
    // Test implementation
}
```

### 2. Mock Dependencies
```java
@MockBean
private I[Service] [service];

@MockBean
private APIResponder apiResponder;

@InjectMocks
private [Controller] [controller];
```

### 3. Setup Method
```java
@BeforeEach
void setUp() {
    this.[controller] = new [Controller](this.[service], this.apiResponder);
}
```

## Test Method Patterns

### 1. Test Method Structure
```java
@Test
@DisplayName("Descriptive test name describing the scenario")
void test[MethodName][Scenario][ExpectedResult]() {
    // Arrange
    [RequestDto] requestObject = new [RequestDto]();
    [ResponseDto] expectedData = new [ResponseDto]();
    APINormalResponse<[ResponseDto]> expectedResponse = new APINormalResponse<>(expectedData);
    ResponseEntity<APINormalResponse<[ResponseDto]>> expectedResponseEntity =
        new ResponseEntity<>(expectedResponse, HttpStatus.OK);

    // Mock service calls
    Mockito.when(this.[service].[method](requestObject)).thenReturn(expectedData);
    Mockito.when(this.apiResponder.respond(expectedData, "Success message"))
        .thenReturn(expectedResponseEntity);

    // Act
    ResponseEntity<?> response = this.[controller].[method](requestObject);

    // Assert
    assertThat(response).isEqualTo(expectedResponseEntity);
    Mockito.verify(this.[service]).[method](requestObject);
    Mockito.verify(this.apiResponder).respond(expectedData, "Success message");
}
```

### 2. Test Naming Conventions
- **Test Class**: `[Controller]Tests`
- **Test Method**: `test[MethodName][Scenario][ExpectedResult]()`
- **Display Name**: Descriptive sentence explaining what the test does
- **Naming Style**: Use PascalCase for scenario and expected result parts (no underscores)

### 3. Test Method Sections
- **Arrange**: Setup test data, mocks, and expectations
- **Act**: Execute the method under test
- **Assert**: Verify the results and mock interactions

## Request/Response Patterns

### 1. GET Operations
```java
@Test
@DisplayName("Get [entity] by id successfully")
void testGet[Entity]ByIdValidIdReturnsEntity() {
    // Arrange
    Integer entityId = 1;
    [Entity]ResponseDto expectedEntity = new [Entity]ResponseDto();
    APINormalResponse<[Entity]ResponseDto> expectedResponse = new APINormalResponse<>(expectedEntity);
    ResponseEntity<APINormalResponse<[Entity]ResponseDto>> expectedResponseEntity =
        new ResponseEntity<>(expectedResponse, HttpStatus.OK);

    Mockito.when(this.[service].get[Entity]ById(entityId)).thenReturn(expectedEntity);
    Mockito.when(this.apiResponder.respond(expectedEntity, "[Entity] fetched successfully"))
        .thenReturn(expectedResponseEntity);

    // Act
    ResponseEntity<?> response = this.[controller].get[Entity]ById(entityId);

    // Assert
    assertThat(response).isEqualTo(expectedResponseEntity);
    Mockito.verify(this.[service]).get[Entity]ById(entityId);
    Mockito.verify(this.apiResponder).respond(expectedEntity, "[Entity] fetched successfully");
}
```

