### 1. Request DTO Creation
```java
// Simple request DTO
[Entity]RequestDto requestObject = new [Entity]RequestDto();
requestObject.setField1("value1");
requestObject.setField2("value2");

// Request DTO with collections
BulkRequestDto requestObject = new BulkRequestDto();
requestObject.setIds(Arrays.asList(1, 2, 3));
```

### 2. Response DTO Creation
```java
// Simple response DTO
[Entity]ResponseDto expectedEntity = new [Entity]ResponseDto();
expectedEntity.setId(1);
expectedEntity.setName("Test Entity");

// List response DTO
List<[Entity]ResponseDto> expectedEntities = Arrays.asList(
    new [Entity]ResponseDto(), new [Entity]ResponseDto());
```

## Error Handling Test Patterns

### 1. Exception Testing
```java
@Test
@DisplayName("Handle [exception] when [scenario]")
void test[Method][Scenario]Handles[Exception]() {
    // Arrange
    [RequestDto] requestObject = new [RequestDto]();
    String errorMessage = "Error occurred";
    APIErrorResponse expectedError = new APIErrorResponse("ERROR", errorMessage, null);
    ResponseEntity<APIErrorResponse> expectedResponseEntity =
        new ResponseEntity<>(expectedError, HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST);

    Mockito.when(this.[service].[method](requestObject))
        .thenThrow(new [Exception](errorMessage));
    Mockito.when(this.apiResponder.respondWithError(errorMessage, HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST))
        .thenReturn(expectedResponseEntity);

    // Act
    ResponseEntity<?> response = this.[controller].[method](requestObject);

    // Assert
    assertThat(response).isEqualTo(expectedResponseEntity);
    Mockito.verify(this.[service]).[method](requestObject);
    Mockito.verify(this.apiResponder).respondWithError(errorMessage, HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST);
}
```

## Best Practices

### 1. Test Organization
- Group related tests in the same test class
- Use descriptive test names and display names
- Follow the AAA pattern (Arrange, Act, Assert)
- Keep tests independent and isolated

### 2. Mock Usage
- Mock all external dependencies
- Use `@MockBean` for Spring components
- Verify important mock interactions
- Don't over-verify trivial interactions

### 3. Test Data
- Use realistic test data
- Create helper methods for complex test data
- Use constants for repeated values
- Keep test data minimal but sufficient

### 4. Assertions
- Use AssertJ for fluent assertions
- Test both positive and negative scenarios
- Verify response structure and content
- Check HTTP status codes

### 5. Coverage
- Test all public controller methods
- Test different input scenarios
- Test error handling paths
- Test edge cases and boundary conditions

## Common Patterns Summary

### 1. Standard Test Structure
```java
@Test
@DisplayName("Clear description of what is being tested")
void testMethodNameScenarioExpectedResult() {
    // Arrange - Setup test data and mocks
    // Act - Execute the method under test
    // Assert - Verify results and interactions
}
```

### 2. Mock Verification Pattern
```java
// Verify service interaction
Mockito.verify(this.[service]).[method](expectedParameters);

// Verify API responder interaction
Mockito.verify(this.apiResponder).respond(expectedData, "Expected message");
```

### 3. Response Assertion Pattern
```java
// Verify complete response
assertThat(response).isEqualTo(expectedResponseEntity);

// Additional specific assertions if needed
assertThat(response.getStatusCode()).isEqualTo(HttpStatus.OK);
```

These rules ensure consistent, maintainable, and comprehensive controller unit tests across the timesheet microservice.
